From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Marcel Ritter <marcel@linux-ng.de>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 20:39:11 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] [winpr,wlog] journald appender: flush after each message

The journald appender opens its stream with sd_journal_stream_fd() and
fdopen(), and writes each message with one fprintf. A stream on a
socket is fully buffered by stdio, and the appender never flushes it:
messages reach journald only when about 4 KiB (the socket's
st_blksize) have piled up, or when the process exits. A server that
logs little, such as freerdp-proxy between connections, shows its
lines minutes late in the journal, and the lines still in the buffer
are lost when the process is killed (SIGKILL, a crash, systemd's stop
timeout).

Flush after each message, as the file appender does. Each message then
reaches journald in one write, right away and in order with the other
messages of the process.
---
 winpr/libwinpr/utils/wlog/JournaldAppender.c | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/winpr/libwinpr/utils/wlog/JournaldAppender.c b/winpr/libwinpr/utils/wlog/JournaldAppender.c
index b0d468b..126aa23 100644
--- a/winpr/libwinpr/utils/wlog/JournaldAppender.c
+++ b/winpr/libwinpr/utils/wlog/JournaldAppender.c
@@ -122,6 +122,10 @@ static BOOL WLog_JournaldAppender_WriteMessage(wLog* log, wLogAppender* appender
 		WINPR_PRAGMA_DIAG_IGNORED_FORMAT_NONLITERAL(void)
 		fprintf(journaldAppender->stream, formatStr, prefix, cmessage->TextString);
 		WINPR_PRAGMA_DIAG_POP
+		/* The stream is fdopen()ed on the journal socket, so stdio buffers it fully:
+		 * without a flush a message reaches journald only once the buffer is full or
+		 * the process exits, and is lost if it is killed. */
+		(void)fflush(journaldAppender->stream);
 	}
 	return TRUE;
 }
